description:
## Caller contract risk: caller can construct wire-invalid JSON via the public \`extra\` map.

The new \`pub extra: serde_json::Map<String, serde_json::Value>\` field is reachable for direct mutation. Nothing prevents a caller from inserting a key whose name matches a typed field on the same struct (e.g. \`addr.extra.insert(\"email\", json!(\"override@example.com\"))\` on an \`EmailAddress\`).

When such a struct is serialized, \`#[serde(flatten)]\` emits the typed field first and then the flattened map's entries verbatim — there is no dedup pass. The wire output therefore contains two entries with the same JSON key.

### Concretely demonstrated

A minimal repro on serde 1 / serde_json 1:

```rust
struct A {
    email: String,
    #[serde(flatten, default, skip_serializing_if = \"serde_json::Map::is_empty\")]
    extra: serde_json::Map<String, serde_json::Value>,
}

let mut a = A { email: \"real@example.com\".into(), extra: Map::new() };
a.extra.insert(\"email\".into(), json!(\"override@example.com\"));
let s = serde_json::to_string(&a).unwrap();
// s = {\"email\":\"real@example.com\",\"email\":\"override@example.com\"}

let _: A = serde_json::from_str(&s).unwrap();
// thread 'main' panicked: duplicate field \`email\` at line 1 column 35
```

Result: the crate's own \`Serialize\` impl produces JSON that the crate's own \`Deserialize\` impl rejects. RFC 8259 §4 calls duplicate keys \"unpredictable\"; downstream consumers will pick either value with no guarantee which.

### Affected types (in this diff)

Every type that received an \`extra\` field is affected: \`EmailAddress\`, \`EmailAddressGroup\`, \`EmailHeader\`, \`EmailBodyValue\`, \`EmailBodyPart\`, \`Email\`, \`MailboxRights\`, \`Mailbox\`, \`Address\`, \`Envelope\`, \`DeliveryStatus\`, \`EmailSubmission\`, \`Disposition\`, \`Mdn\`, \`MdnSendRequest\`, \`MdnSendResponse\`, \`MdnParseRequest\`, \`MdnParseResponse\`, \`Identity\`, \`SieveScript\`, \`SearchSnippet\`, \`Thread\`, \`VacationResponse\`.

### Why callers will hit this

The doc comment on every \`extra\` field describes it as a catch-all for vendor / site / private extension fields — without saying \"do not put keys matching the struct's typed field names here\". A caller programmatically forwarding key-value pairs from a vendor extension namespace will not check for collisions, and the failure surface is silent: serialize succeeds, the wire is malformed, and the next deserialize either picks the wrong value or throws \`duplicate field\` deep inside a transport stack.

### Two ways to harden

1. **Doc-only**: every \`extra\` field doc gains an explicit warning: \"Inserting a key whose name matches a typed field above produces wire-invalid JSON with duplicate keys; the resulting value will not round-trip through this crate's Deserialize impl.\" This is the cheapest fix.
2. **Type-level**: hide the field behind \`pub fn extra(&self)\` / \`pub fn extra_mut(&mut self)\` accessors that validate insertions against the known typed-field set, or replace \`Map<String, Value>\` with a newtype that enforces the invariant. This is a forever-API choice and matches the workspace's \"validate on construction\" stance elsewhere (e.g. \`Id::new_validated\`).

The current pub-field shape locks the workspace into option 1.

### Cross-references

- Workspace AGENTS.md \"Extras-preservation policy for vendor/site fields\" specifies \`pub extra\` and \`serde_json::Map<String, Value>\` — this contract concern is workspace-wide, not local to jmap-mail-types.
- bd JMAP-zx3s.4 covers test gaps; the duplicate-key case is one of the missing tests (would fail loudly today and document the contract).
